External R packages (installed by the user inside R; loaded via rpy2)
[Format: Name (CRAN / Bioconductor) - URL]
--------------------------------------------------

These R packages are required at runtime by COAST's feature-selection
module (coast/feature_selection.py), which calls into R via rpy2 to run
GENIE3 gene-network inference. They are NOT bundled with COAST; users
install them themselves inside R, see GENIE3_via_rpy2.txt for the full setup procedure.

GNU General Public License v2 or later (https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html)
  * GENIE3 (Bioconductor) - https://bioconductor.org/packages/GENIE3/
      [Random-forest-based gene regulatory network inference. GPL-2 or
       later, per its Bioconductor metadata.]
  * doRNG (CRAN) - https://cran.r-project.org/package=doRNG
      [Reproducible parallel random-number generation. GPL-2 or later.]

GNU General Public License v2 (https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html)
  * doParallel (CRAN) - https://cran.r-project.org/package=doParallel
      [Parallel backend for foreach. GPL-2.]

These packages are GPL-family. Because COAST does not bundle them and
they are loaded from the user's own R installation only when the user
opts into the feature selection step (which itself requires the
optional rpy2 extra), their GPL obligations apply to the user's local
environment, not to redistribution of COAST.

--------------------------------------------------
External Java component (py-tetrad and the Tetrad jar)
[Format: Name (artifact) - URL]
--------------------------------------------------

COAST invokes Tetrad's causal-discovery algorithms through the py-tetrad
JPype bridge. py-tetrad and tetrad-current.jar are NOT distributed with
COAST. Users must install py-tetrad themselves from
https://github.com/cmu-phil/py-tetrad. They are listed here for
transparency about the full runtime dependency chain that a user will
have on their own machine after following COAST's install instructions.

MIT License (https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT)
  * py-tetrad (cmu-phil/py-tetrad) - https://github.com/cmu-phil/py-tetrad
      [The Python wrapper code itself.]

GNU General Public License v2 (https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.html)
  * Tetrad (edu.cmu.tetrad, distributed inside py-tetrad as
        tetrad-current.jar) - https://github.com/cmu-phil/tetrad
      [The Java causal-discovery library invoked through py-tetrad.
       Tetrad is GPLv2. COAST does NOT bundle this jar; it is loaded
       from the user's own py-tetrad installation at runtime, so the
       GPLv2 obligations apply to the user's local py-tetrad checkout,
       not to redistribution of COAST itself.]

The Tetrad jar embeds the following third-party Java libraries; their
licenses are reproduced inside the jar under META-INF/ and license/:

  Apache License, Version 2.0
    * FastDoubleParser - https://github.com/wrandelshofer/FastDoubleParser
    * Various Apache Commons / utility libraries (see jar META-INF)

  BSD-style License
    * BigInt arbitrary-precision integers (bigint-LICENSE)
    * W3C DOM / SAX XML parsers (license/LICENSE.dom-*.txt,
      license/LICENSE.sax.txt)

  Eclipse Public License v1.0 / Common Public License v1.0
    * JUnit (LICENSE-junit.txt) - https://junit.org/

----
NOTE: Although we try to keep this list current, transitive dependencies
pulled in by pip/conda may add additional packages. The latest information
about installed third-party libraries and their licenses can be obtained
with:

  pip install pip-licenses
  pip-licenses --format=plain --with-urls


For the Java side, the licenses bundled inside tetrad-current.jar can be
listed with:

  unzip -l /path/to/py-tetrad/pytetrad/resources/tetrad-current.jar | \
      grep -iE 'license|notice'
